          content:
            '<p>The moral reputation of the US military was severely damaged by revelations of war crimes:</p>\n        <ul>\n          <li><strong>The Event:</strong> On 16 March 1968, US troops from Charlie Company, led by <strong>Lieutenant William Calley</strong>, were on a Search and Destroy mission in the village of My Lai. Finding no VC, the frustrated soldiers massacred between <strong>347 and 500 unarmed Vietnamese civilians</strong>, mostly women, children, and elderly men.</li>\n          <li><strong>The Cover-Up and Exposure:</strong> The military covered up the atrocity for over a year, reporting it as a victory. In late 1969, investigative journalist <strong>Seymour Hersh</strong> exposed the story, and graphic photographs were published in Life magazine, shocking the public and triggering the largest political protest in US history (500,000 marching in Washington DC).</li>\n          <li><strong>The Trial:</strong> In 1971, only Lt. Calley was convicted of murder. He was sentenced to life imprisonment, but President Nixon intervened, resulting in Calley serving just over 3 years under house arrest, which disgusted many Americans who felt he was a scapegoat to protect higher officers.</li>\n        </ul>',
